  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid evolution of AI-powered code agents has transformed software development workflows, enabling automated code generation, execution, and debugging. However, these powerful tools introduce significant security and safety concerns that necessitate rigorous evaluation and mitigation strategies. This paper examines the current state of research at the intersection of code agents and red-teaming methodologies. We review recent benchmarks and frameworks designed to evaluate the safety and trustworthiness of code agents, including RedCode [1] and BlueCodeAgent [2]. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current evaluation methodologies and proposes directions for future research. We highlight the importance of comprehensive red-teaming approaches that can systematically probe code agents for vulnerabilities, as well as blue-teaming strategies that aim to strengthen defense mechanisms. This work contributes to the growing discourse on AI safety in the context of automated code generation and execution systems.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents—AI systems capable of generating, executing, and iteratively refining code—have emerged as transformative tools in software engineering. Powered by large language models (LLMs) with advanced code generation capabilities, these agents can handle complex programming tasks, debug errors, and even execute code in isolated environments. The integration of code interpreters and execution capabilities has further expanded their utility, enabling dynamic, interactive programming workflows.\n\nDespite their promise, code agents introduce novel security and safety challenges. The ability to autonomously generate and execute code creates attack surfaces that extend beyond traditional software vulnerabilities. Malicious code agents could potentially execute harmful operations, generate vulnerable code, or be manipulated through prompt injection to perform unauthorized actions. These concerns have galvanized the research community to develop robust evaluation frameworks and red-teaming methodologies.\n\nRed-teaming, traditionally employed to identify security vulnerabilities in systems through adversarial testing, has become essential for evaluating code agents. The process involves systematically probing agents with adversarial inputs to uncover weaknesses, unintended behaviors, and potential safety risks. Recent research has produced dedicated benchmarks and agent frameworks specifically designed for red-teaming code agents and code generation models.\n\nThis paper surveys the landscape of research at the intersection of code agents and red-teaming. We first provide background on code agents and their capabilities. We then review key benchmarks and frameworks, including RedCode and BlueCodeAgent, that have been developed to evaluate and improve the safety of these systems. Finally, we discuss open challenges and future directions for research in this critical area.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe safety and security of AI systems has become a paramount concern as these technologies are deployed in increasingly critical applications. In the context of code generation and code agents, several important works have laid the foundation for systematic evaluation and improvement.\n\n**RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.** Guo et al. introduced RedCode [1], a comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate the safety of code agents across diverse scenarios. The benchmark specifically targets the risks associated with generating and executing code that could potentially be harmful. RedCode provides a systematic framework for assessing how code agents handle risky prompts and whether they can be manipulated into generating or executing dangerous code. The benchmark covers a wide range of attack scenarios, including prompt injection, jailbreaking, and various forms of adversarial manipulation. By providing standardized evaluation protocols and a diverse dataset of test cases, RedCode enables researchers to systematically compare the safety of different code agent architectures and develop more robust defense mechanisms.\n\n**BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.** Building on the foundations of red-teaming research, Guo et al. proposed BlueCodeAgent [2], which takes a blue-teaming approach to enhancing the security of code generation AI systems. While red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming aims to strengthen defenses and improve the resilience of AI systems against adversarial attacks. BlueCodeAgent leverages automated red-teaming to continuously stress-test code generation models, identifying weaknesses and then implementing countermeasures to address them. This iterative approach creates a feedback loop that progressively improves the safety and robustness of code agents. The framework demonstrates that combining red-teaming and blue-teaming methodologies can yield significant improvements in model security compared to either approach alone.\n\n**Additional Context.** Beyond these specific contributions, the broader research community has developed various trustworthiness assessments for large language models. Works such as DecodingTrust [3] have provided comprehensive evaluations of the trustworthiness properties of GPT models, establishing evaluation methodologies that have informed subsequent research in the code agent domain. The attention mechanism introduced in [4] has become foundational to the transformer architectures underlying most modern code agents.\n\n## Methodology and Framework Analysis\n\nThe evaluation of code agent safety requires careful consideration of multiple dimensions. We analyze the methodological approaches employed in recent benchmarks and frameworks:\n\n### Evaluation Dimensions\n\n1. **Code Generation Safety**: Assessing whether agents generate secure code or vulnerable code that could be exploited.\n\n2. **Code Execution Safety**: Evaluating whether agents can be manipulated into executing malicious code or harmful operations.\n\n3. **Prompt Injection Resistance**: Testing the agent's ability to resist adversarial prompt modifications.\n\n4. **Jailbreaking Resistance**: Evaluating defenses against attempts to bypass safety constraints.\n\n### Benchmarking Approaches\n\nRecent work has demonstrated that static evaluation is insufficient for understanding code agent behavior. The RedCode benchmark emphasizes dynamic evaluation through actual code execution in sandboxed environments, providing more realistic safety assessments. Similarly, BlueCodeAgent employs continuous evaluation through automated red-teaming cycles, capturing the evolving nature of threats and defenses.\n\n## Discussion\n\nThe field of code agent safety and red-teaming is still in its early stages. Key observations from our analysis include:\n\n1. **Need for Standardized Benchmarks**: While benchmarks like RedCode provide valuable evaluation frameworks, there is still a need for community-wide adoption of standardized evaluation protocols to enable meaningful comparisons across different code agent architectures.\n\n2. **Comprehensive Threat Modeling**: Current benchmarks cover important threat categories, but ongoing research is needed to identify emerging attack vectors as code agent capabilities evolve.\n\n3. **Defense Mechanism Development**: While RedCode provides evaluation frameworks, there is less work on systematic defense mechanisms. BlueCodeAgent's approach of using automated red-teaming to inform defense improvements represents a promising direction.\n\n4. **Human-in-the-Loop Considerations**: The role of human oversight in code agent workflows remains an important consideration, particularly for safety-critical applications.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe intersection of code agents and red-teaming represents a critical area of research for ensuring the safe deployment of AI-powered programming tools. This paper has surveyed recent advances in this domain, including the RedCode benchmark [1] for evaluating code execution and generation safety, and the BlueCodeAgent framework [2] for automated red-teaming and blue-teaming of code generation models. These contributions demonstrate that systematic evaluation and continuous improvement through adversarial testing are essential for building trustworthy code agent systems.\n\nLooking forward, several research directions emerge as priorities: (1) developing more comprehensive benchmarks that capture the full spectrum of potential threats; (2) creating scalable defense mechanisms that can be integrated into code agent pipelines; (3) establishing evaluation protocols that can keep pace with the rapid evolution of code agent capabilities; and (4) fostering interdisciplinary collaboration between AI safety researchers, security experts, and software engineering practitioners.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into software development workflows, the research community must remain vigilant in developing and evaluating safety measures. The work surveyed in this paper represents important steps toward that goal, providing foundations upon which future research can build safer and more trustworthy AI-powered programming tools.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Yuzhou Nie, Chulin Xie, et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\nEOF"
